INSERT OR IGNORE INTO posts (slug, title, excerpt, body, cover_image, category, author_name, published_at, status) VALUES
  ('check-out-retailnow-this-july', 'Check Out RetailNOW This July', 'Restaurants are excited to get back up and running this summer, and so are businesses adjacent to foodservice. Whether you run a restaurant or provide the technology for them, reconnect with the industry this July at...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Restaurants are excited to get back up and running this summer, and so are businesses adjacent to foodservice. Whether you run a restaurant or provide the technology for them, reconnect with the industry this July at the RetailNOW show."},{"type":"p","text":"Why? Because the industry is emerging from the COVID-19 pandemic very different from how it went in. Everyone could use networking opportunities, new partners, and the latest technology to get back on their feet. The RetailNOW show is a great place to learn more."},{"type":"p","text":"WHERE? Nashville, TN at Gaylord Opryland Events Center"},{"type":"p","text":"WHEN? July 25th through July 27"},{"type":"p","text":"If you’re interested in learning more about the restaurant industry, upcoming trends, marketing, online services, and payment systems, then check out this event at the end of July. There’s something new and interesting for everyone."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by RODNAE Productions from Pexels"},{"type":"h2","text":"The RetailNOW 2021 Show"},{"type":"p","text":"Their website marks the event as, “The retail IT channel’s trade show, education conference, and networking event is back - bigger and better than ever!”"},{"type":"p","text":"It’s true; you can get all sorts of things out of the event depending on what you’re looking for. With hundreds of booths, you’ll find all sorts of information pertinent to your business."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Educational information that affects the core functions of your restaurant, for the better."},{"type":"p","text":"2. Industry experts talking about how they handle various aspects of modern restaurant ownership. From ghost kitchens to cloud technology and everything in between, staying up-to-date on the latest industry trends keeps you in competition with the best."},{"type":"p","text":"3. Meet other vendors to form partnerships and networking opportunities. Support systems will be important during the recovery period post-COVID. This also presents an opportunity to run joint promotions or events with other vendors."},{"type":"p","text":"4. Recognizing both up-and-comers to the industry and superstars who are setting the bar for great leadership."},{"type":"p","text":"This year the RPSA will present Vendor Awards of Excellence. Resellers vote for prominent members of the retail technology industry to honor their hard work, particularly VARs (Value-Added Resellers) and ISVs (Independent Software Vendors) trendsetting for the industry at large."},{"type":"h2","text":"Join eatOS This July"},{"type":"p","text":"eatOS will see you at booth. Sign up as an attendee or open a booth, and come say hi at the event!"},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #Restaurant #RestaurantBusiness"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_a2d59c0386814d93a595c70cf6cefda0~mv2.jpg', 'Kitchen Operations', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-28', 'published'),
  ('eatos-partners-at-the-retailnow-show-in-nashville', 'eatOS Partners at the RetailNOW Show in Nashville', 'Are you ready for the RetailNOW 2021 show? This weekend, July 25th through July 27th, resellers will gather in Nashville, TN for the industry event of the summer. eatOS has their booth and is ready to meet with other...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Are you ready for the RetailNOW 2021 show? This weekend, July 25th through July 27th, resellers will gather in Nashville, TN for the industry event of the summer. eatOS has their booth and is ready to meet with other industry professionals, restaurant representatives who want to know more about what we do here, and anyone else who wants to stop by and say hi!"},{"type":"p","text":"The event takes place at the Gaylord Opryland Events Center. eatOS is proud to join some of our merchant partners at the event."},{"type":"h2","text":"eatOS Partners and RSPA"},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by Emma Dau on Unsplash"},{"type":"p","text":"The Retail Solutions Providers Association, more commonly known as RSPA, hosts the RetailNOW show each year. Some of our outstanding merchant partners even sponsor the organization! We’re proud to work with Blue Star, a platinum sponsor; Ingenico Group and Star Micronics, both silver sponsors; Epson, a bronze sponsor; and so many other great companies."},{"type":"p","text":"Meet us and our outstanding partners at the event this upcoming weekend. Check out our booths, keynote speakers sharing their industry expertise, new networking connections, the latest products sweeping the marketplace, and so much more that will help any business flourish."},{"type":"p","text":"Star Micronics will also host a podcast at the trade show, and you can expect to hear a special guest from our very own eatOS team, so be sure to tune in!"},{"type":"h2","text":"See You at RetailNOW 2021!"},{"type":"p","text":"Expect to see hundreds of first-time attendees, companies, and countries all around the world represented at this year’s show. Especially with people excited to get out and rejoin the business world after over a year, people are excited to network at the event, even more than any other reason for attending."},{"type":"p","text":"eatOS is ready to meet partners, professionals and prospective new relationships at the RetailNOW show in Nashville this coming weekend. We’ll also be debuting our new eatOS Point of Purchase at the event, so be sure to stop by booth and see our latest restaurant POS device!"},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RetailNOW #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_5199d8c7ecb34250993c773d32247324~mv2.jpg', 'Restaurant Insights', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-26', 'published'),
  ('healthy-food-trends-to-expect-this-summer', 'Healthy Food Trends to Expect This Summer', 'The COVID-19 pandemic has caused a massive shift in how Americans dine out. Newfound concerns center on health and wellness, especially when it comes to what we consume. That’s why sales have risen for foods that do...', '[{"type":"p","text":"The COVID-19 pandemic has caused a massive shift in how Americans dine out. Newfound concerns center on health and wellness, especially when it comes to what we consume. That’s why sales have risen for foods that do something for our bodies. Whether they improve mental health, boost energy, or boost the immune system and digestive health, people want to nourish themselves. This is especially true while fear lingers about a dangerous respiratory virus. That’s why probiotic food and drinks are selling more as well, like kombucha, yogurt, and kefir. Healthy food is getting more popular."},{"type":"p","text":"But it’s not just that. More Americans started diets in 2020, too. According to the International Food Information Council, 43% were on diets last year. That beats the few years previous, possibly as part of the wave of people expanding their horizons during quarantine. Dietary-restricted menus draw in people who follow vegan, vegetarian, gluten-free, keto, or paleo diets, to name just a few with waves of new followers."},{"type":"p","text":"Plant-based menus are also on the rise. Tofu, grains, chickpeas, and similar foods are replacing ingredients in popular dishes. For example, trade beef for beans in that burger, and your new target audience will follow. These alternatives aren’t just good for business and customers’ health; they’re good for the planet, too."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by amoon ra on Unsplash"},{"type":"h2","text":"What Restaurants Can Do"},{"type":"p","text":"Even if they don’t end up ordering the healthy option, customers like to know it’s on the menu anyway. According to a 2019 Technomic study, 34% of diners would prefer a restaurant just because of its healthy options."},{"type":"p","text":"It doesn’t have to be as complicated as overhauling the whole menu. You can offer ingredient alternatives to appease customers and keep costs down. Have a list of non-meat burgers or gluten-free buns. Milk alternatives have multiplied too, like coconut, almond, and soy. Swapping out ingredients is a simple way to fulfill this rising expectation amongst consumers."},{"type":"p","text":"Even just offering healthy side options works, too. You don’t have to exclusively pare down the menu to healthy food choices in order to effectively draw in those new customers. Keep that side of fries and onion fries, and just add grilled veggies or soup as another."},{"type":"h2","text":"Healthy Food Trends This Summer"},{"type":"p","text":"In the coming months, the warm weather will keep driving people to try those in-season fruits and vegetables. That will coincide with the rise in digital ordering, like takeout and delivery via QR code or a branded app. For the best restaurant experience, you need a smart and efficient Point of Sale system."},{"type":"p","text":"eatOS offers an all-in-one cloud-based eco-system exclusively for restaurants. From Point of Sale to Contactless Ordering, Pay & Order at Table, Kitchen Display System, Guest Facing Display, Online Ordering APP / Website and Workforce Management there is everything to help a restaurant succeed no matter the size. Click Here to Schedule a demo to learn more about how your restaurant can make a smooth transition into the world of restaurant technology made simple."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_32140846756843ea9e6adc08f7c28baf~mv2.jpg', 'Restaurant Growth', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-25', 'published'),
  ('how-to-support-black-owned-businesses-nationwide', 'How to Support Black-Owned Businesses Nationwide', 'As peaceful protests over the death of George Floyd and against police brutality flourish around the country, many people want to find additional ways to support people of color in their communities. Some protest,...', '[{"type":"p","text":"As peaceful protests over the death of George Floyd and against police brutality flourish around the country, many people want to find additional ways to support people of color in their communities. Some protest, others donate, many share information and petitions, but people are also furthering community goals by supporting black-owned businesses in their area."},{"type":"p","text":"Black-owned companies are some of the ones hit hardest by the COVID-19 pandemic which has ravaged the U.S. for months, and they continue to struggle as partial reopening leaves nonessential businesses like restaurants operating at only partial capacity. Since the beginning of June, people have started looking for new ways to support black-owned initiatives in their communities."},{"type":"h2","text":"Lists of Black-Owned Businesses"},{"type":"p","text":"Resources for black-owned businesses as well as compilations of local, black-owned restaurants tailored for cities all across the country are appearing all over social media. With these on hand, people can use their takeout and delivery services while sheltering in place against COVID-19. Some of these lists cover broad areas, including restaurants across an entire state, whereas others stay specific to certain cities or smaller communities, and these circulate more locally."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Los Angeles"},{"type":"p","text":"This list of Los Angeles-based, black-owned restaurants has a plethora of information on each one so that people can easily find whatever will fill their cravings. The information provided includes which neighborhood the restaurant operates out of, their phone numbers and websites, social media, any gift cards and merchandise you can purchase, and of course the type of cuisine they serve there."},{"type":"p","text":"They cover all kinds of restaurants including barbecue, breakfast, vegan and vegetarian, Jamaican, seafood, soul food and more."},{"type":"p","text":"1. New York"},{"type":"p","text":"New Yorkers have made a variety of lists, all catering to different communities. This list covers over 200 restaurants all over New York City, while this one specifies the ones in Queens. Black-Owned Brooklyn runs an Instagram that highlights people, places and products throughout Brooklyn; they also have a website where you can subscribe to get more updates on the community as a whole."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Atlanta"},{"type":"p","text":"This guide compiled by Atlanta Beltline Hates You displays the neighborhoods, addresses, owners, Instagrams and websites of over one hundred black-owned restaurants in Atlanta, GA. They also include a list of other resources, a Google Maps sheet to direct community members straight to their restaurant of choice, and even have a submission form so Atlantans can help the creator expand the list."},{"type":"p","text":"1. D.C."},{"type":"p","text":"Black food writer and advocate Anela Malik curated black-owned restaurants in the DMV, also known as the Washington Metropolitan Area that includes D.C. as well as parts of Maryland, Virginia and West Virginia. A stand-out aspect of this particular list is that it specifically focuses on restaurants open now during COVID-19. Malik frequently updates the information to guarantee its continued relevance and accuracy."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Denver"},{"type":"p","text":"303 Magazine crafted a list of nearly 300 black-owned businesses in and around the Denver area. They don’t just name restaurants but also include companies across a variety of industries, as well as other resources that support the black community in Denver."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Louisville"},{"type":"p","text":"This blog operated by members of the Yelp Louisville Elite Squad not only names but reviews the best black-owned restaurants and food businesses in Kentucky. People have to apply to join the group, ensuring that top quality reviewers recommend the best of the best."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Minnesota"},{"type":"p","text":"Back where these protests started, Minnesotans are compiling black-owned restaurants and bars in the state. Along with this, they urge consumers to patronize these businesses all year long, not just during Black History Month or when there’s a large amount of civil unrest. Additionally, they’re asking readers to reach out and let them know about more businesses to supplement and bolster their list."},{"type":"p","text":"All around the U.S., people are making catalogues like these. Check out more cities who have compiled black-owned businesses in their communities including Austin, TX; Chicago, IL; New Jersey; Portland, OR; Seattle, WA and more. People want to support black-owned businesses, which is why these lists and many more like them are being created and shared across all different social media sites."},{"type":"p","text":"Order from these businesses, promote them on your own platforms and share these lists with your friends and neighbors. Generating extra business for black-owned restaurants is an easy way to support local community members and try new, great food in your neighborhood."},{"type":"h2","text":"Download Directories"},{"type":"p","text":"As support for black-owned restaurants rises en masse, so does loyalty to initiatives that help people find and order from them. Unlike community-organized lists, these are often apps that assemble local black-owned businesses, update regularly, and let people find and order food directly from their mobile device."},{"type":"p","text":"A few different directory-style apps have launched over the years, all sharing the same goal of promoting small, black-owned businesses around the country."},{"type":"p","text":"1. EatOkra"},{"type":"p","text":"This black-owned business directory app started because the founders wanted to give back to their community in a substantial way. As they put it, “Nothing embodies this idea of community more than the act of feeding someone or coming together to eat or be fed” which is why they’re constantly growing and maintaining their directory to help restaurants and community members flourish and get their needs met together."},{"type":"p","text":"1. The Black Wallet"},{"type":"p","text":"At The Black Wallet, they seek to uplift black entrepreneurs by promoting all types of businesses, their owners and the greater black community. Their efforts don’t end with their app and online directory: They also detail all of their services and offer educational courses that teach young entrepreneurs the skills they need to succeed."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Black Nation"},{"type":"p","text":"This business discovery app supports all kinds of black-owned businesses and entrepreneurs. Business owners can download the app and become featured for a chance to support and promote their company. Black Nation also has an Instagram where they promote businesses and important causes."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Support Black Owned"},{"type":"p","text":"This blog directory compiled black-owned businesses from all over the world so that people in every country can help support black entrepreneurs in their communities. They accept donations, offer great deals and feature businesses on their website. They also have an app that anyone can download and easily find nearby restaurants. Help take the movement worldwide with Support Black Owned."},{"type":"p","text":"These are just a few of the many directory apps coming to help support black-owned businesses in local communities. Since June began and the push to support black-owned businesses has grown in fervor, these apps and others like them have seen spikes in downloads anywhere from 50% to over 4000%."},{"type":"p","text":"On top of helping support black business owners, young entrepreneurs can also find resources to guide them to success."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Food Education Fund"},{"type":"p","text":"They have job training, paid internships and other entrepreneurship and enrichment programs that help prepare black high schoolers for a career in the food service industry."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Black and Brown Founders"},{"type":"p","text":"This initiative provides community, education and access to black and Latinx entrepreneurs. They supply them with resources that will help them succeed."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Black Female Founders"},{"type":"p","text":"They foster entrepreneurship and invention by providing business owners with the resources and information necessary to succeed."},{"type":"p","text":"1. Okra Academy"},{"type":"p","text":"A subset of The Okra Project, Okra Academy teaches kitchen basics and recipes to black trans people so they can enrich their personal lives in the kitchen. They also train to become better chefs so they can pursue a career in the food service industry, and the Project even provides homecooked meals to those in the black trans community who need it."},{"type":"p","text":"While these resources and programs spread through social media, news outlets like Forbes and Glamour are also promoting black-owned businesses and endeavors like these all around the country. Especially considering the mass pandemic affecting the entire world, independent restaurants all need community support."},{"type":"p","text":"Support black business owners by donating to and ordering directly from local restaurants. Whether compiled by community members on a Google doc or assembled in an app, you can support these local businesses easily just by ordering dinner."},{"type":"h2","text":"Today, Tomorrow and Every Day Beyond"},{"type":"p","text":"Supporting black lives shouldn’t end when the protests do. Don’t only shop at these restaurants because Black Lives Matter is trending. Instead, use these resources to get into the habit of supporting black-owned businesses and continue doing so long after this particular movement goes dormant."},{"type":"p","text":"Combatting systemic racism is an everyday process. Doing something as simple as shifting, at least partially, from patronizing chain establishments to supporting independently owned businesses, specifically black-owned independent businesses, is a very easy adjustment in your day to day life. Support people of color in your community, particularly black business owners working hard to serve their neighbors in the midst of protests for their right to live and the COVID-19 pandemic on top of that. The fight doesn’t end herebut it’s a good place to start."},{"type":"p","text":"#Community #FoodService"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_adee9251f7cb4ddb87876f8b2a96f527~mv2.jpg', 'Online Ordering', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-21', 'published'),
  ('meet-the-eatos-pop-at-retailnow-2021', 'Meet the eatOS POP at RetailNOW 2021', 'The RetailNOW Show is happening right now in Nashville. It began yesterday on July 25th and will continue on through tomorrow. eatOS is excited to have a booth set up there to connect with other industry...', '[{"type":"p","text":"The RetailNOW Show is happening right now in Nashville. It began yesterday on July 25th and will continue on through tomorrow. eatOS is excited to have a booth set up there to connect with other industry representatives, learn from experts and do so much more. We’re also introducing eatOS POP."},{"type":"p","text":"Image by Julia Schwab from Pixabay"},{"type":"h2","text":"Introducing eatOS POP, Out Now!"},{"type":"p","text":"This tradeshow will be the debut of our new device. POP, or Point of Purchase, is a handheld device with all the same functionality of a full-size Point of Sale. Servers can use its portability to offer tableside service to guests. It also connects to the rest of your restaurant system so orders go straight back to the kitchen. Thus, the cooks can get started immediately which shortens table turn times and increases how many parties you can seat each day. All that adds up to more revenue, just by reducing how long it takes to run orders to the back of the house."},{"type":"p","text":"The POP also gives your team more flexibility to offer a premiere guest experience. It takes all kinds of payment methods and splits the check, so every party member pays for their meal however works best for them. No matter how they choose to complete the transaction, eatOS POP makes it easy, safe and secure. Our cloud-based system encrypts and stores data so your customers never have to worry."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo courtesy of eatOS"},{"type":"p","text":"Stop by booth to see the debut in person!"},{"type":"h2","text":"eatOS Partnerships at RetailNOW"},{"type":"p","text":"Some of our fantastic merchant partners sponsored the RetailNOW show, too. Star Micronics is also attending. Check out their podcast, where you might recognize one of our own eatOS team as a guest!"},{"type":"p","text":"We’re thrilled and honored to be part of a one-of-a-kind industry event as big as this trade show, with so many leaders and experts in attendance. Stop by and say hi, and check out the debut of our new eatOS Point of Purchase too."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant #CloudTechnology"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_b37ce3c78e1046a884d49b2339f4acdb~mv2.jpg', 'Point of Sale', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-20', 'published'),
  ('restaurant-trends-that-will-define-2021', 'Restaurant Trends that Will Define 2021', 'The foodservice industry spent most of 2020 learning to adapt for COVID-19, but now they’re starting to adjust for what comes next. As customers get used to dining out again, new restaurant trends will overtake what’s...', '[{"type":"p","text":"The foodservice industry spent most of 2020 learning to adapt for COVID-19, but now they’re starting to adjust for what comes next. As customers get used to dining out again, new restaurant trends will overtake what’s currently popular and your restaurant has to be ready to change too."},{"type":"p","text":"Here are five restaurant trends to look out for in 2021."},{"type":"h2","text":"Restaurant Trends on the Rise"},{"type":"h2","text":"1\\. Prioritizing Comfort"},{"type":"p","text":"Six-foot social distance regulations. Mask mandates. Constant sanitization and COVID-19 infections causing temporary shutdowns. Right now, safety concerns reign."},{"type":"p","text":"As normalcy returns, people will seek the comfort and relaxation that they haven’t felt at a restaurant for over a year. Creating a relaxed space for customers to spend time together will be a massive draw moving forward. Warm hospitality goes hand-in-hand with a comfortable seating area to get customers to de-stress and enjoy their time at your restaurant."},{"type":"h2","text":"2\\. Virtual Experiences"},{"type":"p","text":"Most likely, your target base has gotten comfortable online during this pandemic. The world went digital, so why shouldn’t your restaurant follow? Draw in online audiences with whatever your restaurant offers that nobody else can. What sets you apart from the competition? Maybe you’ll team up with famous chefs to share cooking tips with people via video chat. Offer virtual classes where your own back of house teaches a few staple recipes. This is the perfect time to put your creativity to work."},{"type":"p","text":"You can also expand your online services. Start a store with merchandise, gift cards, and meal kits, whatever appeals to your target audience best. New sales avenues can optimize your online presence for as long as you need to prioritize virtual services."},{"type":"h2","text":"3\\. Waste Management"},{"type":"p","text":"Food waste can account for so much of your unnecessary expenses. Although this has always been true, now that COVID-19 has changed the marketplace so thoroughly, it’s time to reassess even if you prioritized waste reduction before."},{"type":"p","text":"Check your sales reports for key insights into how the pandemic has affected inventory and profit. Your average order and ticket sizes have likely changed, as well as the price of ingredients and how long they last before you need to restock. Updating this information reduces unnecessary labor costs and tells you how to appropriately price your own dishes to get a good ROI. For example, you might find that seasonal menus and local ingredients better constrain prices in an unstable market."},{"type":"h2","text":"4\\. Healthy Alternatives"},{"type":"p","text":"Nutrition concerns are on the rise, as consumers expect allergy- and diet-friendly options and favor functional ingredients too. Soy and vegan choices, as well as plant-based alternatives, thrive as customers seek more vitamins, probiotics, and immune-boosting capabilities. Even drinks menus are seeing trends toward healthy options, with low-calorie alternatives, hard seltzer, and hard kombucha gaining popularity this past year."},{"type":"p","text":"A food’s origin story matters. People want sustainably sourced ingredients, local suppliers, and animal and workers’ rights. Stuck at home, they’ve also expanded their palates to try new foods from all over the world. Experimenting with new dishes could pay off while people are feeling adventurous."},{"type":"h2","text":"5\\. Contactless Services"},{"type":"p","text":"Social distance has necessitated touch-free services. Customers crave a safe dining experience whether they choose sit-down or delivery. From curbside pickup services to contactless payment options, touch-free solutions are growing to the point where even dine-in services have contactless alternatives now. With QR codes, guests can scan to pull up a digital menu on their smartphone where they can order can pay from their seats."},{"type":"p","text":"Prioritize sanitation and distance as you create new habits in accordance with the changing marketplace. Keep customers engaged and happy to order from you with contactless services. This trend is set to continue long-term so build up your digital marketing and online ordering platforms in preparation to rely on them for a while, even after we move past the pandemic."},{"type":"h2","text":"Following Restaurant Trends"},{"type":"p","text":"Upgrade your restaurant technology so you can grow and change with the times. The best businesses run on smart Point of Sale systems."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #CoronavirusPandemic #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantRecovery #Covid19Pandemic #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_cff4e3ec866f4734aeaa7d817258515d~mv2.jpg', 'Restaurant Growth', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-18', 'published'),
  ('restaurants-give-back-does-charity-drive-sales', 'Restaurants Give Back: Does Charity Drive Sales?', 'Money has become a bigger concern for the restaurant industry than ever before, because of COVID-19. Customers are less financially secure, likewise for businesses, and the community as a whole hasn’t fared much better....', '[{"type":"p","text":"Money has become a bigger concern for the restaurant industry than ever before, because of COVID-19. Customers are less financially secure, likewise for businesses, and the community as a whole hasn’t fared much better. When guests do spend money, they want to know it goes to a worthwhile cause. Because of this, as you get more involved with charity, sales will soar."},{"type":"p","text":"Why is that?"},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by RODNAE Productions from Pexels"},{"type":"h2","text":"Customers, Charity, and the Middleman"},{"type":"p","text":"More fundraising organizations have cropped up in response to the pandemic, and a lot of people need help. Spread the word to your audience and they’ll get the resources they need, while customers feel better about where their money goes."},{"type":"p","text":"Given that funds are tight, people look for businesses that share their values. For example, environmentally-focused customers will want to align themselves with green companies. Your restaurant’s charity sets you apart as worthwhile. This boost to your reputation underscores how the company mission connects to your core values. This matters; according to Accenture, a consulting service, in 2018 two-thirds of customers reported that they would rather buy from businesses that share their beliefs. This leads to more customer loyalty which leads to more spending."},{"type":"p","text":"Bring customers and charity together in your restaurant. It fosters goodwill in the community and your reputation will soar."},{"type":"h2","text":"What and How to Donate"},{"type":"p","text":"Identify who your restaurant wants to help. It depends on your community. Perhaps there are a lot of families in need; if so, work directly with outreach organizations that take your leftovers at the end of the day. This has the dual benefit of giving restaurant-quality ingredients to those who wouldn’t otherwise be able to afford it, so they can feed their family healthy meals. Meanwhile, restaurants generate a lot of food waste. Eliminate some by donating your overstock so it goes to good use."},{"type":"p","text":"Alternatively, get food to those in need by hosting food drive events or partnering up with food banks directly. Keep visible collection boxes out so people can drop off cans the next time they visit or offer coupons for every donation to incentivize charity."},{"type":"p","text":"Money goes far for these kinds of organizations, too. Write on each menu that a portion of the check goes to charity, or host fundraisers to donate to third parties that distribute to the needy. People want to help their neighbors, and your restaurant can create avenues to do so."},{"type":"h2","text":"Direct Charity for Restaurants"},{"type":"p","text":"Don’t rely on third parties. There are things your business can do without help. For example, donate spare PPE to places that still need more, like hospitals or reopening schools. You might also host dinners for hungry community members. Team up with other restaurants in the area to cut down on costs. Open yourself up to donations to help fund these endeavors, too. If you can’t host in person because of the current labor shortage, or for some other reason, consider delivering directly to homeless shelters, schools and other organizations that can handle a gathering."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by cottonbro from Pexels"},{"type":"p","text":"As the pandemic continues, social media remains the most effective way to spread the word about new endeavors. As you become more involved with charity work, use your pages in creative ways, such as hosting polls to see what fundraisers your guests want, or promoting dinner events in advance."},{"type":"p","text":"Togetherness, flexibility and adaptability will get us through the next, tumultuous months. Restaurant recovery will not be easy. Lean on your network to find creative solutions to the ongoing problems plaguing your neighborhood. There’s no better way to show commitment to the community than by lending a helping hand."},{"type":"p","text":"Make sure your charity efforts run smoothly. eatOS offers an all-in-one cloud-based eco-system exclusively for restaurants. From Point of Sale to Contactless Ordering, Pay & Order at Table, Kitchen Display System, Guest Facing Display, Online Ordering APP / Website and Workforce Management there is everything to help a restaurant succeed no matter the size. Click Here to Schedule a demo to learn more about how your restaurant can make a smooth transition into the world of restaurant technology made simple."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #CoronavirusPandemic #PointofSaleforRestaurants #Covid19Pandemic #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_1f83f01f195248e680523c1f757af61b~mv2.jpg', 'Marketing & Loyalty', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-14', 'published'),
  ('where-are-pos-trends-heading-next', 'Where Are POS Trends Heading Next?', 'Every aspect of the restaurant industry has changed during COVID-19, even its technology. Advanced Point of Sale systems now run restaurants all over the country. Trends indicate that POS will stay on this track for a...', '[{"type":"p","text":"Every aspect of the restaurant industry has changed during COVID-19, even its technology. Advanced Point of Sale systems now run restaurants all over the country. Trends indicate that POS will stay on this track for a long time."},{"type":"p","text":"Here’s what the industry can expect to see out of POS trends."},{"type":"p","text":"Photo by Mark OFlynn on Unsplash"},{"type":"h2","text":"1\\. POS in More Businesses"},{"type":"p","text":"With the pandemic finally ending, more people see the value in upgrading their systems. Previous to COVID-19, restaurants were much warier of new tech. They worried whether their customers and staff could smoothly adapt. They wondered if the cost of investment would ever match ROI. However since the pandemic caused a massive sweep in restaurants digitizing their processes, it’s now status quo for new businesses to open with top-of-the-line equipment from the start."},{"type":"p","text":"Restaurants aren’t the only businesses in hospitality that noticed these upsides. You’re more likely to see smarter Point of Sales in coffee shops, bars, and all kinds of places besides fine dining. POS capable of more than a standard register is commonplace in a post-pandemic world."},{"type":"h2","text":"2\\. Widening to New Consumers"},{"type":"p","text":"Flexibility has been key this year. Coronavirus regulations shift as swiftly as customer preferences, so the businesses that thrive are the ones that adapt. For many restaurants, that meant turning to entirely new customer touchpoints. Delivery and takeout largely replaced face-time with your guests. Now everyone is comfortable with these channels. Thus some habits will remain long after the world reopens."},{"type":"p","text":"What generates business? Self-service options are extremely popular. Even when people move off the apps to dine-in seating, restaurants can incorporate digitization even there. For example, QR codes enable customers to pull up the menu, order, and pay right from their seats and on their phones. More and more restaurants are also choosing to invest in a Kiosk, especially considering the labor shortage that currently has a stranglehold on businesses. Digitization helps them contend with more foot traffic as things open back up."},{"type":"p","text":"Still, these changes aren’t all good news. They may experience slight delays because they need more regular sanitation, for example. Temperature checks, and collecting info at the door in case of contact tracing, takes a lot of time. Don’t let that deter you though: Restaurants actually benefit from gathering this information. Use it to build out your customer profiles and develop a stronger understanding of your best patrons’ habits and preferences. This will strengthen marketing campaigns and ultimately pull in more money, too!"},{"type":"h2","text":"3\\. A POS for the Whole Restaurant"},{"type":"p","text":"As we mentioned above, Point of Sales aren’t just cash registers anymore. Now they do so much more. The POS system powered by eatOS, for example, covers your commercial kitchen, servers, online orders and everywhere else you need to run your business. From tablets that submit orders tableside, to a branded mobile app made just for you, restaurants can streamline their operation from order to checkout."},{"type":"p","text":"#eatOS #PointofSaleforRestaurants #RestaurantBusiness #Restaurant"}]', 'https://static.wixstatic.com/media/a77ed6_e484e7ce260c4c50aadec48d69161dcf~mv2.jpeg', 'Point of Sale', 'eatOS Staff', '2023-04-13', 'published');
